# config/kernel/Kconfig.sync — Locks, futexes, RCU and lock-wait instructions

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Spin-wait (arch/api spin.rs, every ISA)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config SPIN_WAIT_RELAX_SPINS
    int "Spin-wait: relax polls before the wait hint"
    range 0 65536
    default 0 if ARCH_AARCH64
    default 8 if ARCH_RISCV64 && HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 16 if ARCH_RISCV64
    default 64 if ARCH_X86_64
    default 16
    help
      `SpinWait::wait_while` re-reads the word this many times with
      `cpu_relax` (pause / yield / PAUSE) between reads before it takes
      the ISA's wait hint (Zawrs wrs.nto, LDAXR+WFE, UMWAIT) when the
      hint is in use; without a hint it is the poll count per round and
      changes nothing. 0 goes straight to the hint (Linux arm64's
      `__cmpwait`; aarch64's default, where WFE wakes on the store with
      no polling). Higher: a lower wake latency for short holds, more
      bus reads and power while waiting. Costs no RAM; about 4
      instructions per poll.

choice
    prompt "SpinLock implementation"
    default SPINLOCK_IMPL_MCS if !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED && NR_CPUS >= 4
    default SPINLOCK_IMPL_MCS if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED && NR_CPUS >= 2
    default SPINLOCK_IMPL_TTAS
    help
      What `SpinLock<T>` (crates/core/sync) is under the same API; no call
      site changes. Both use one 32-bit word, no sub-word atomic.

      mcs: Linux's qspinlock (crates/core/sync/src/qspinlock.rs). Waiters
      queue in arrival order, each spinning on its own CPU's node, so a
      waiter's wait is bounded by the holds of the CPUs ahead of it (at
      most NR_CPUS - 1) and no CPU is starved. Uncontended acquire: one
      fetch-or and a branch (amoor.w.aq on riscv64, LSE LDSETA or LL/SC on
      aarch64; LOCK CMPXCHG on x86_64), as many instructions as ttas;
      release: a byte store. RAM: NR_CPUS * 64 bytes of queue nodes; about
      1 KiB of out-of-line text.

      ttas: test-and-test-and-set (an exchange, then wait reading the word).
      Cheapest under no contention, but a CPU that releases and retakes the
      lock can keep it while others spin: no bound on a waiter's wait.

      Defaults: mcs on edge and fleet with NR_CPUS >= 4; on embedded with
      NR_CPUS >= 2 (the real-time profile, where a bounded wait is the
      contract and the nodes cost 256 bytes at 4 CPUs); ttas otherwise (one
      CPU never queues, and with 2-3 CPUs edge/fleet favour code size).

    config SPINLOCK_IMPL_TTAS
        bool "ttas: test-and-test-and-set"
    config SPINLOCK_IMPL_MCS
        bool "mcs: queued (qspinlock), FIFO"
endchoice

config SPINLOCK_MCS_NODES
    int "Queued SpinLock: nesting levels per CPU"
    range 1 4
    default 4
    help
      Queue nodes per CPU (SPINLOCK_IMPL = mcs), one per level of nested
      contended acquisition on one CPU: task, deferred work, interrupt,
      NMI-like. A CPU deeper than this waits on a trylock loop instead of
      queueing (no FIFO for that acquisition). 16 bytes per node per CPU;
      4 fill one cache line. Lower only to save RAM on a kernel that never
      takes a SpinLock from interrupt context while one is contended.

config SPINLOCK_MCS_PENDING
    bool "Queued SpinLock: pending bit"
    default y
    help
      With SPINLOCK_IMPL = mcs, the first waiter behind a holder spins on
      the lock word itself (one fetch-or) instead of taking a queue node:
      cheaper hand-off with one contender, as in Linux. Off: every waiter
      queues (one node write and a tail CAS more per contended acquire).

config RCU_QSBR
    bool "Read-copy-update, quiescent-state based (QSBR)"
    default n if NR_CPUS = 1
    default y
    help
      Lock-free readers for kernel tables read far more often than they
      change (crates/core/sync/src/qsbr.rs): a reader takes no lock, and a
      writer frees what it replaced only after every CPU has passed a
      quiescent state (a context switch, a return to user mode, idle).
      The capability-table reads (`cap_store::get`, SYS_CAP_INFO) run
      this way. Owner decision Q2: QSBR only (no sleepable readers), and
      callbacks never run on the RT CPUs (RCU_NOCBS_CPUS).
      Costs: 64 bytes per CPU (NR_CPUS), one kernel task (the callback
      task) and, on a kernel entry from user mode or from idle, one atomic
      add and a fence; on a return to user mode, one atomic add with
      interrupts masked around it (about 10 instructions per syscall); at
      a context switch, one store.
      Off: not compiled. The capability reads take the table's SpinLock as
      before, and no entry, switch or idle path carries an instruction.
      Default off with one CPU, where a lock is never contended and the
      per-syscall instructions buy nothing.

config RCU_NOCBS_CPUS
    hex "RCU: CPUs that never run RCU callbacks (the RT CPUs, a mask)"
    default 0x0
    help
      Bit n set: CPU n never runs an RCU callback; the callback task is
      pinned to the lowest CPU whose bit is clear (CPU 0, with a boot
      warning, if every possible CPU's bit is set). Set the real-time
      cluster's CPUs here so a batch of frees never lands in their
      latency. A CPU in user mode is quiescent with or without a tick
      (rule F5), so an RT CPU running one user task never holds a grace
      period. Default none: no board here designates RT CPUs yet. No
      cost: a constant read at boot.

config RCU_STALL_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "RCU: a grace period older than this is a stall (ms)"
    range 10 600000
    default 500 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4000 if BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    default 2000
    help
      A grace period that has not ended this long after it started is
      reported once: the stall counter (`qsbr::stalls`, printed by the
      callback task, failing the running ktest) names the first CPU that
      has not passed a quiescent state. A CPU stuck in kernel code with
      preemption off, or a hook that stopped reporting (the ktest canary
      `rcu-idle-qs-skip`), shows here. Higher in a development build
      (BUILD_TYPE_DEV: the QEMU defconfigs, whose timer is host wall time
      under TCG; the build type, not the board, keys it). No cost: a
      compare in the waiter's poll.

config RCU_CALLBACK_PRIORITY
    int "RCU: callback task priority (outside the RT band: 12..30)"
    range 12 30
    default 20
    help
      The task that waits for grace periods and runs RCU callbacks (the
      frees) on the lowest CPU outside RCU_NOCBS_CPUS. Never RT: a free is
      never urgent, and it sleeps between checks. Lower numbers run
      first; the default sits with the io_ring worker. No cost.

config RCU_GP_POLL_MS
    int "RCU: grace-period poll interval of a waiter (ms)"
    range 1 1000
    default 1 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 2
    help
      A waiter (`synchronize_rcu`, the callback task) sleeps this long
      between checks of the CPUs' counters while a grace period is open,
      and not at all otherwise. Shorter frees sooner at one timer wake per
      interval while a grace period is open; longer delays frees. One
      check reads one word per online CPU.

config A64_WFET_TICKS
    int "aarch64: WFET deadline (generic counter ticks)"
    range 100 100000000
    default 2500
    help
      The deadline each WFET step is given (A64_WFXT): the longest one
      wait step sleeps when no store to the lock word and no event
      arrives, after which the word is re-read. 2500 ticks is about 40
      us at QEMU's 62.5 MHz counter and 100 us at 24 MHz. Lower: more
      re-reads of a word nobody writes. Unused without FEAT_WFxT.
      Visible on every ISA so the constant always exists.

config A64_TLBIRANGE_MIN_PAGES
    int "aarch64: smallest shootdown that uses a TLB range invalidation (pages)"
    range 2 4096
    default 4
    help
      A shootdown of at least this many pages issues TLBI RVAE1IS /
      RVAAE1IS (A64_TLBIRANGE): at most five operations for any length,
      against one per page. Shorter runs stay per-page, where the
      range form saves nothing. Unused without FEAT_TLBIRANGE. Visible
      on every ISA so the constant always exists.

config X86_UMWAIT_TSC_TICKS
    int "x86_64: UMWAIT deadline (TSC ticks)"
    range 1000 100000000
    default 100000
    help
      The TSC deadline each UMWAIT step is given (X86_WAITPKG): the
      longest one wait step sleeps when no store to the line and no
      interrupt arrives, after which the word is re-read. 100000 is
      Linux's IA32_UMWAIT_CONTROL default cap (about 30-50 us). Lower:
      more re-reads of a word nobody writes; higher: bounded only by the
      MSR cap. Unused without WAITPKG. Visible on every ISA so the
      constant always exists.

config X86_UMWAIT_C02
    bool "x86_64: UMWAIT may enter C0.2 (deeper, slower wake)"
    default y if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default n
    help
      n: C0.1 (ECX[0]=1), the faster wake a lock waiter wants. y: C0.2,
      lower power and a slower wake (the sibling thread gains more), for
      a power-bound board. Unused without WAITPKG.

config FUTEX_SHARED
    bool "Linux shared futexes on shm words keyed by (region, offset)"
    default y
    help
      A Linux futex without FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG on a word of a shm region
      the caller maps is filed in the futex table under the region's
      object id and the word's offset (crates/core/sched/src/futex_table.rs),
      the key the native notify calls use, so two processes that map the
      region at different addresses meet on it. n: every Linux futex is
      process-private (two processes sharing a word do not meet); the
      native notify calls are unchanged.
      Cost: no RAM; one shm mapping lookup (a scan of the shm table under
      its lock) on each Linux futex call without FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG.

config FUTEX_REQUEUE
    bool "FUTEX_REQUEUE and FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE"
    default y
    help
      The Linux futex ops that wake some waiters of one word and move the
      rest to another without waking them (a condition variable's
      broadcast moves its waiters onto the mutex: one runs, not the herd).
      n: both answer -ENOSYS, and a C library falls back to waking all.
      Cost: no RAM; ~300 B of kernel text.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wait graph and its clients (wave 15 IF: N7 PI core, N8 kernel mutex,
# N10 PI futex). Interfaces in crates/core/sync.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config WAIT_GRAPH
    bool "Wait graph: one priority-inheritance core for mutex, PI futex and IPC"
    default y
    help
      crates/core/sync/src/waitgraph.rs (N7): every object a task blocks
      on while another task owns it (kernel mutex, PI futex, IPC call,
      driver proxy) records its waiters by urgency, and a bounded
      chain walk boosts the owner to its most urgent waiter (a deadline
      donor passes its absolute deadline). A cycle is refused with EDEADLK.
      y on every profile (the protocol is priority inheritance). n is for a
      profile that uses a priority ceiling or a static schedule: every
      client keeps its counted-donation path (proxy, fast IPC), the
      kernel Mutex still sleeps and excludes but boosts nobody, and the PI
      futex answers ENOSYS.
      Stage 1 serialises the graph under one lock (interrupts off for the
      length of one walk); per-object locks come later, same API.
      Cost: 24 B per blockable object and a per-task PI record (32 B x
      MAX_TASKS: effective attribute, waiter link, owned set, boost
      floor); nothing on an uncontended acquire; one walk of at most
      PI_MAX_DEPTH steps per contended block or release. n: zero bytes.

config KMUTEX_ADAPTIVE_SPIN
    int "Kernel mutex: spin-hint iterations while the owner runs, before sleeping"
    range 0 100000
    default 0 if NR_CPUS = 1
    default 40 if ARCH_X86_64
    default 200
    help
      A contended Mutex spins at most this many spin-hint iterations
      (pause / yield / Zihintpause) while its owner is executing on
      another CPU, then blocks on the wait graph. 0 never spins (the only
      sensible value on one CPU). x86_64's PAUSE costs about 140 cycles on
      current cores, so fewer iterations cover the same time as the
      cheaper riscv64 and aarch64 hints. Raise it when sections are short
      and context switches dominate; lower it under RT load (a spinning
      task holds its CPU). Cost: no RAM; at most this many iterations of
      CPU time per contended acquire.

config KMUTEX_STEAL_RULE
    bool "Kernel mutex: after a release only a more urgent task may overtake the top waiter"
    depends on WAIT_GRAPH
    default y
    help
      After a contended Mutex unlock, the word stays marked (no owner,
      WAITERS) while a waiter is queued in the wait graph, so a newcomer
      cannot take it with the one-CAS fast path. A task takes it only if
      it is the top waiter, or strictly more urgent than the top waiter
      (wait-graph PiAttr: Stop > DL by earlier deadline > RT by priority
      > fair > idle), as Linux rt_mutex lock stealing. n: any newcomer may
      take the lock ahead of the woken waiters (Linux's non-RT mutex),
      which favours throughput over the waiters' latency. Cost: no RAM;
      one graph-lock round trip and one top-waiter read per contended
      acquire after a release; nothing on the uncontended paths.

config FUTEX_PI
    bool "PI futexes: FUTEX_LOCK_PI/UNLOCK_PI/TRYLOCK_PI and requeue-PI"
    depends on WAIT_GRAPH
    default y
    help
      crates/core/sync/src/pi_futex.rs (N10): Linux's PI futex word
      protocol (TID | FUTEX_WAITERS | FUTEX_OWNER_DIED) on the wait graph,
      owner-died through the robust list, and CMP_REQUEUE_PI /
      WAIT_REQUEUE_PI. n answers ENOSYS as today. Cost: one pi_state
      (about 48 B) per contended PI word, from a pool of MAX_TASKS.

config FUTEX_PI_EXIT_RETRY_US
    int "PI futex: back-off (us) before a locker retries a word whose owner is exiting"
    range 1 100000
    default 200 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 100
    help
      crates/core/sched/src/futex.rs (N10c, owner decision 10-10, Linux
      handle_exit_race): a FUTEX_LOCK_PI/TRYLOCK_PI that finds the word's
      owner mid-exit never makes it the owner; it drops the futex table
      lock, sleeps this long so the exit hands the word over, and tries
      again (deadline and signals honoured). Only that rare window pays
      it. Lower trades CPU of a high-priority locker spinning on the exit
      for latency; raise it on slow cores where an exit takes longer.
      Read only with FUTEX_PI y. Cost: no RAM; one timer sleep per retry.
